  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 AM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nToday through tonight: Areas of fog are focused over the lower \nPine Belt region early this morning, and this will persist to \naround sunrise. Otherwise, expect another day of dangerous heat\nand have maintained the heat advisory that is in effect for\nwestern/northern portions of the area. The cyclonic flow \nresponsible for the enhanced storm coverage Friday will be \nrelaxing a bit today, but there will still be enough to support \nisolated to scattered diurnal storms and slightly less dangerous\nheat mainly in the Pine Belt region. \n\nSunday into Sunday night: Quick amplification of the upper level \ntrough over the eastern CONUS will sent a front toward our \nforecast area. There will be some added compressional heating \nahead of the boundary with H850 temperatures in the low 20s deg C,\nand this will result in continued elevated dangerous heat levels,\nand most likely another heat advisory. In addition, a marginal \nsevere risk has emerged for Sunday afternoon/evening as scattered \nstorms should initiate ahead of the front in an airmass \ncharacterized by steep low to mid level lapse rates. This will \nsupport severe wind gusts in the more intense storms. If storm \ndevelopment becomes more imminent, we could certainly see the \nrisk level increase to slight (2 of 5) as there is recent \nprecedence for storms to overachieve with the hotter days and \nanomalously hot H850 temps. \n\nMonday through Friday: A stubborn subtropical ridge will remain\nplanted over the Gulf Coast states, so don't expect signs for early\nfall weather to show up any time soon. Instead, we can expected \nadditional limited to elevated dangerous heat threats and reduced\nrainfall coverage mostly limited to southern portions of the \narea. /EC/",
